        # ReleaseNotes of this module
        ReleaseNotes = 'v0.15.0: Bash-compatibility release (XFAIL marks in the bash spec suite 140 -> 79). BREAKING: paths a script sees use forward slashes on Windows ($PWD, ~, $HOME, mktemp, realpath print C:/x; input still accepts C:\x and /c/x); bash code no longer resolves PowerShell aliases (gci, iex, r are command not found, 127, unless a function or program has the name; use the full cmdlet name); a nested shell sees exported variables only (x=1; sh -c "echo $x" prints an empty line; use export; a plain prog still sees all). Background jobs: output streams as the job runs, in launch order, with bounded memory; a job reads its own copy of the variables; a job sleep counts from its &. New: kill -SIG $$ and signals sent to a shell pid reach its trap; hash keeps the bash table; BASH_LINENO; --rcfile; a session whose hosts are all busy runs the command on a private host (PSBASH_EXEC_OVERFLOW_MS). Fix: pasted Unicode quote and dash marks no longer break the emitted PowerShell; line tools join a piece without its newline into the next line (printf a | sed); a while-read pipe stage reads lines as they are written; set -e ends a failing pipe stage; exit, break and errexit stop at subshell boundaries; pipefail after && and ||; an assignment sets PIPESTATUS; stopping a child ends its whole process tree; a 2>&1 child keeps its write order; a nested bash streams and is never timed out; a record keeps its trailing empty lines; base64 and od stream endless input; /dev/* names are never opened as files; file redirects write as output is produced; readlink and realpath canonicalize like GNU; kill -l prints the bash signal table; {a,} is one word; command and nohup run #! scripts; command not found carries the bash line prefix. macOS: scripts no longer die with exit 138 (SIGUSR1 is left to the .NET runtime); stat, signals and ioctl are decoded per OS and arch. Known issues: docs/known-issues.md. v0.14.5: Bash-parity pass: 189 oils spec cases fixed (XFAIL 316 -> 127). Fix: "$@" inside a function reached a command as one joined word (cat "$@" with two files failed) and dash options passed to a function (f -i x) broke grep/head "$@". Fix: a stopped command no longer kills the shared host; a nested ps-bash joins its enclosing session instead of starting a second daemon; ps-bash host list, and host gc removes only orphaned hosts. New: fc, piped bash -i runs as an interactive session, history -a/-w/-r, PROMPT_COMMAND, \D{fmt} in prompts, compgen -X/-A user/-A command, set -v/-n, BASHOPTS, umask -p, mapfile -O, printf %(fmt)T honors TZ, LC_ALL=C counts bytes, kill/wait jobspecs, ulimit soft/hard limits, redirect target globs. Also: GLOBIGNORE, POSIX ERE in =~, octal and invalid arithmetic values, NUL bytes in $( ) and read, printf %q of invalid UTF-8, sed \xHH, cat /dev/zero, a script with a NUL byte is refused as binary, unterminated quotes are syntax errors, failglob and bad substitutions drop the rest of the line, aliases with here-docs, tilde and globstar inside braces. v0.14.4: Resource-leak pass. Fix: a mktemp file a long script still needed was deleted once any shell ran a process substitution five minutes later. Now removed instead of piling up: socket files left by killed hosts, old module extraction directories (about 5 MB per build, kept while any process uses them), and empty mktemp -d directories older than 10 days. A background-job spare host that failed to start is replaced at once. Fix: aliases in plain PowerShell with Import-Module PsBash ("Unable to find type"); $_ in and after a pipeline; exec {fd}>file allocates a descriptor and >&$fd writes to it; bash -i -c sets HISTFILE; a quoted ] does not close a glob bracket. v0.14.3: Bash-compatibility batch (about 140 fixes, checked against bash 5.2). New: chmod (octal and symbolic modes, -R -v -c -f), od, nohup for background processes that outlive the shell, symbolic umask modes, set -a / allexport, $SHELLOPTS, history in scripts; launcher -e, -u, -o and -O apply to the -c string. Fix: unquoted expansions glued to text split as bash splits them, in arguments, $(...), [ ] and extglob patterns; arrays (sparse slices, element initializers in declare/local, subscripts as words, associative key order); namerefs, unset and local scoping; exit and return statuses, errexit, the ERR trap and errtrace; return inside eval text, source searches PATH; exec on stdin/stdout/stderr and numbered descriptors on a simple command; [ ] and [[ ]] operand rules; redirects to /dev/stdout and /dev/stderr; pushd, popd and dirs; trap DEBUG, CDPATH, the time keyword. Fix: bash -c and bash script.sh run the host bash when no ps-bash launcher is installed beside an embedded module (Linux), and start in the shell directory, not the process directory. v0.14.2: New ExecutableLookup (PsBash.Core): one execvp-style PATH search shared by the interactive shell, test -x, traceroute, tab completion and embedders - directories and files without an execute bit are skipped, so a non-executable file earlier on PATH no longer shadows the real binary (was exit 13 permission denied). find no longer forks stat per entry on Linux/macOS (Owner/Group resolve lazily). 10 more Oils spec cases now pass. v0.14.1: Bash-compatibility batch (about 250 fixes, oracle-verified). New: set -x trace, wait -n, timeout command, local -n namerefs write through, typeset -n/+n, aliases expand at transpile time under expand_aliases, compgen -F/-C/-W/-A, ${v@a/K/k/A}, readonly arrays, terminal tab title follows the running command. Fix: dirs -c/-v/-p, ERR trap LINENO and errtrace. Fix: read (heredoc, -d, -N/-n/-t, REPLY, backslash-newline); parameter expansion (indirect ${!ref}, ${#a[i]}, case-modifying `${v^}`/`${v,,}` with patterns, ${@-w}, negative slices); arrays written in functions reach the caller, per-element operators, unset a[-1]; declare/typeset/readonly/local attributes and declare -p output; `[` / `[[` operand and grouping rules, integer-expression errors; arithmetic edge cases and failing (( )) status; trap signal specs and EXIT traps in subshells; set -e with missing commands (127) and set option handling; alias bodies run as bash, egrep/fgrep; brace and tilde expansion corners; printf 64-bit integers; PIPESTATUS, BASH_VERSINFO, FUNCNAME, LINENO; background jobs see loop variables; rm -rf of the current directory; scripts piped into bash/sh. Release: NuGet publish uses trusted publishing (OIDC). v0.14.0: Remote sessions. New: ps-bash session attach NAME gives a reattachable terminal (tmux-style) - a detached broker owns a PTY and the interactive shell, so the shell and its screen outlive the client; Ctrl-] d detaches, a new client gets the last 1 MiB of output replayed, several clients can attach (--read-only to watch), and session stop ends it. Remote use is ssh -t host ps-bash session attach NAME. Works on Linux, macOS and Windows (ConPTY); the wire protocol is documented in docs/sessions.md. Named sessions (PSBASH_SESSION=NAME) are now stateful by default: variables, cd and functions persist between commands in one pinned runspace (--stateless restores a fresh runspace per command); session list shows each mode. Changed: the Windows named-pipe transport is removed; AF_UNIX sockets are the only transport (a socket path over the OS limit now fails with a message naming TMPDIR). Fix: a shell started under a Windows pseudoconsole no longer inherits the parent redirected stdio (it read EOF and exited with no prompt), so PSBASH_PTY=1 works when ps-bash is launched from a pipe-driven parent. v0.13.0: Release integrity and safety. SECURITY: tar extraction refuses to write through an existing symlink or junction below the destination (it could escape the directory and still report success). New: named durable sessions - ps-bash session start|list|status|stop NAME plus PSBASH_SESSION=NAME give a host that outlives its launchers and runs concurrently with other sessions (docs/sessions.md). New: history lists entries, history -d ID deletes one, history -c erases all (secure delete, WAL truncate, VACUUM); a line starting with a space, or any line while PSBASH_HISTORY_PRIVATE=1, is never recorded; an unopenable history DB degrades to in-memory with a warning. Fix: install.sh and install.ps1 install the complete package (host and runtime files), smoke-test a real command before switching, keep the previous version for rollback, and name an unsupported platform. Release pipeline: binaries are built from one pinned commit, smoke-tested, and published only after the tests pass; dry-run mode rehearses a branch. Test harness: deadlines are checkpoints that extend while the machine is starved. v0.12.0: Native commands now get their piped stdin as raw bytes (no more added \r\n per record, binary input intact) through the new ConvertTo-BashNativeStdin step the transpiler emits ahead of a native command; cat feeds natives the same way. Fix: mv/cp replacing an in-use destination never deletes its own source when the source is named like a leftover (.name.psbash-replaced-*), and a copy that fails part-way restores the original destination instead of losing it. Fix: a pattern with thousands of nested extglob groups ([[ x == @(@(@(...)))]]) is a clean error, not a host-killing stack overflow. Fix: source of a file named Invoke-BashSource.sh works; sourcing an empty file sets $? to 0 as bash does. v0.11.6: Fix (#7): ps-bash -c no longer piles up idle ps-bash-host processes under an AI agent. The warm background host now exits about 5 seconds after the shell or agent session that started it ends, instead of idling for 10 minutes; an agent that ran each command in a fresh shell used to leave one ~65 MB host per command. Warm reuse inside a live session is unchanged. New docs page The Host Daemon explains sessions, when the host exits, and PSBASH_SESSION / PSBASH_PER_INVOCATION / PSBASH_HOST_IDLE_SECS. Fix: a command that stops reading piped stdin early (yes | ps-bash -c ''head -n 1'') no longer exits 125 with "host dropped the connection" on Linux/macOS. Fix: cd ~/dir works on Linux/macOS (the path after ~ was joined with a backslash). Fix: $HOME and ~ follow the calling shell''s HOME when commands share a background host. Fix: release zips report the right version in ps-bash --version (a v0.11.5 zip said 0.10.19). New: on WSL, the first unknown command warns when Windows /mnt directories on PATH make command lookups slow, and says how to turn them off. Older versions: https://github.com/standardbeagle/ps-bash/releases'

    } # End of PSData hashtable

